gc.c: every GC object slot issued and revoked on its own, under MRB_CAPSTONE_GC_SUBLET (4.0.0-rc2).

The third arm of the port (docs/design/mruby-gc-sublet-port-plan.md). Without
the define the preprocessed file is identical to before. With it, a heap page's
1024 80-byte slots are carved from the program's region 1, which the GC holds
linearly: a handle senior to the page, then one split per slot. mrb_obj_alloc
takes a slot (sublet_take: its handle stays in the sidecar, the object gets an
alias bounded to the slot); the sweep frees a dead object through the GC's own
alias and then revokes the slot (sublet_give), after which every stale copy of
that object's capability is dead, and the next object in the slot gets a new
alias under a new handle.

The page header becomes a sidecar from mrb_malloc, and no free slot is read or
written: its state is obj[i] == NULL, its free-list link next_free[i]. The five
sites that read free slots (the sweep, the gray rescan, free_heap,
gc_each_objects, allocation) and heap_p / mrb_object_dead_p, which may be handed
a dead object, answer from the sidecar; the latter locate the slot from the
address as an integer. So does obj_free's fiber case, which walks a dying
fiber's frames and tests each frame's env with heap_p and is_dead: that env may
have been swept earlier in the same sweep, its slot revoked, and the first run
of this arm faulted there reading it (cause 24, obj_free, mrbtest's Enumerator
tests); it now tests and uses the slot through the GC's own alias. Two deviations, both stated in the code: an all-dead page
is kept rather than freed whole (its slots are each revoked), and
mrb_gc_add_region refuses the embedder's buffer. Needs sublet.h on the include
path and a host that grants region 1 (libc-test's LT_GC_REGION_BYTES).
mrb_capstone_gc_sublet_stats reports the arm's own counts (pages, slots issued
and revoked, this file's Sublet primitives).

